WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
Apple is requiring more explicit user action before AI agents can access full disk data on Macs, raising the bar for agent permissions. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 192. Apple is tightening privacy controls on Mac computers as increasingly capable artificial intelligence agents gain access to sensitive user data. The company announced on Friday that it will introduce additional safeguards around its full disk access feature. The change requires more

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.040
explicit user action before an app can obtain broad access to files, messages, mail, and

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:36.620
other data.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:42.960
Apple said that full-disc access is a powerful Mac OS capability that largely bypasses

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.440
the operating system's normal privacy protections.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:51.880
The feature exists in part because certain applications, particularly backup services, need

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.800
broad access to a Mac's data to function properly.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.760
However, Apple said some developers are using the capability in ways that could expose users

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.880
to privacy risks.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.120
As AI agents become increasingly capable and autonomous, the risks associated with this

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.160
level of access will grow substantially.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.080
Apple directly tied the planned changes to the rapid development of autonomous AI agents.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.340
The company said an application with this level of access can potentially reach virtually

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.720
everything stored on a Mac, including files, mail, messages, and browsing history.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.460
Going forward, Apple said it will introduce additional controls so users who want to grant

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.160
an application full disk access must take a more explicit action to do so.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:45.400
Apple shares ended with gains of about 2% in Friday's regular trade, while Meta shares gained 0.3%.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:51.340
In Model Updates, Quen Flash Next now uses Les V-RAM thanks to a new pull request.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.240
The Quen 4 EXP change halves the index or score memory for the model.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.460
The update targets memory usage during inference for the Quen family.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:09.180
Unitry Robotics released Unifold LM-WLA-1.0, their new general-purpose humanoid foundation model.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:15.780
It carries 6 billion parameters trained on approximately 2,500 hours of real robot data.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:22.220
One model handles 64 tasks consisting of 10 whole body and 54 tabletop actions.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.300
It supports parallel grippers and two different dextrous hands.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:32.200
The model shows strong spatial reasoning that beats many open source models on embodied benchmarks.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.740
Architecture starts with Unitry G1 making the bed, loading the washing machine, folding

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.440
clothing clothes and sorting objects.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.940
An agentic model from Microsoft called Frog Nano targets the GPU poor.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.260
Frog Nano is derived from Quinn-Slasch Quinn 3.5-4B.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.980
It inherits the dense 32-layer hybrid gated delta net and gated attention architecture.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:26.320
The model is further trained using reinforcement learning on approximately 1,500 synthetic

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.520
SWE task environments generated and calibrated against the evolving policy using task pilot.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.420
Training uses the five-tool leaf harness and executable test-based rewards over complete

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:44.720
multi-turned coding trajectories. The additional post-training targets long horizon repository navigation,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:51.740
debugging, code editing, test execution, and patch generation. Performance is sensitive to the

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:57.980
leaf harness and test quality. Training data are Python heavy and primarily English. Generated

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:03.660
patches may be incorrect or insecure despite passing available tests. Benchmarking used

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:15.380
Alama.cpp-based expert streaming setup for Quinn3.8-flash-next177b in UDIQ3 underscore XXS on Windows.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:23.200
The benchmark reached 11.5 tokens per second, up from roughly 7 tokens per second on the inherited setup.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.460
Normal conversations reached 14 to 15 tokens per second.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:37.520
A long coding prompt generated 4,892 tokens at 10.15 tokens per second and produced a working single file snake game.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:51.520
Hardware consisted of RTX 5070 12GB, 32GB DDR4-2400, Ryzen 5 5600 GT, on PCIe Gen 3 under Windows.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:58.040
Main gains came from fixing Windows I.O.Q depth issues, using one file handle per worker,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:03.040
and building a page-locked hot expert tier. In agent and tool developments,

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.600
ML subgen generates subtitles in 45 languages entirely on your own machine.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.220
It detects the language of every stretch of speech rather than per file, so mixed language

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:15.120
material works.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.840
It runs two speech recognizers on everything and has a local LLM reconcile them.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.000
It prefers existing human work to machine inference.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:30.560
Embedded subtitle tracks are used before the audio is, including OCR of bitmap PGS tracks

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:36.320
on Blu-ray remixes, and it only listens when there is nothing to read. Every feature has a

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:44.340
measured accuracy in the readme. It runs on a 24 GB RTX A5000 with profiles for 16, 12 and 8

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:51.820
GB cards. It wants 16 to 32 GB of system RAM depending on the profile and is storage hungry with

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:58.880
30 to 65 GB of models. It is slow when it has to listen, roughly real-time per target language on the

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:07.400
test card. Megacapibara is a purpose built engine for RTX 5090, focused on Quinn 3.827b.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.360
It beats ninfer by roughly twice in decodespeed for both single and multi-tasks. It reaches

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:21.340
up to 650 tokens per second in small bursts, and 2,600 tokens per second if stars align,

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:27.780
with 12 slot server pure coding answer. Custom kernels switch dynamically for short versus

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:34.320
long context. It includes D-flash 2 and confidence scheduling from D-Spark plus draft trees.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.560
Wates come with attached metadata that runs benchmark and compares at every setting

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:47.440
to original BF-16 weights. The engine shows lost KL and top 1% when switching KV to 4-bit

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:53.120
or extending with yarn. It supports up to 1 million context and 12 slots for agents.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:59.740
options. Proper cash management keeps pre-fill almost unused after initial job start. Unified

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:06.880
context swaps tasks to RAM instantly in 0.03 seconds when context runs out. In practical

